  "text_to_speech": "kingdom animalia it is also called animal kingdom animal kingdom includes multicellular organisms with differentiated tissues that means specialized tissues doing different jobs examples nervous tissue blood tissue muscular tissue animals do not have chlorophyll so they feed heterotrophically that means they depends on others like herbivores when they feed on grasses or carnivores when they feed on praise dear just remind yourself with fungi when they feed on dead living organisms or its remains they also considered heterotrophs animal cell do not have cell wall animals are able to move from place to place to find their food to mate or escaping from predators with exception see animone and corals our cecil stay fixed in place animal kingdom is divided into two main divisions number one invertebrates which includes animals without backbone number two vertebrates which includes animals with backbone invertebrates is divided into many phyla but only we will study one of them that is called arthropoda we will classify arthropoda in the next slide while vertebrates only includes one phylum that is called chordata phylum chordata is subdivided into five classes according to the body covering class fish with moist scales class amphibia with naked scaleless skin class reptiles with dry scales class birds with feathers class mammals with hair or fur phylum arthropoda arthro means jointed pods mean legs so arthropoda is literally means animals with joint of legs phylum arthropoda is divided into four classes according the number of legs class insect with three pairs of joint of legs that means six legs class arachnid with four pairs of joint of legs that means eight legs class crustacea with more than four pairs of jointed legs class maryapita each segment of their body has one or two pairs of joint of legs dear please notice that animals that belongs to phylum and elite looks like animals of arthropoda but they have no jointed legs",
